WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.610
Here’s a puzzle from our computer club.

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.650
A chatbot gave Zainab a bus time

3
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.210
that was totally wrong.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.630
Her phone’s assistant set

5
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.770
her alarm perfectly.

6
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:12.730
What’s the difference?

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.100
Today: what’s inside an AI agent,

8
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.060
and how to read what it does.

9
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.210
A chatbot is a language model

10
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.230
that only writes text back.

11
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.050
It guesses likely words, so it can

12
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.270
sound sure and still be wrong.

13
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.050
An agent can also use tools:

14
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.850
small programs like a timetable,

15
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.650
the weather or an alarm.

16
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.910
An agent works in a loop.

17
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.060
You make a request, and the model writes

18
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.900
a short plan, then a tool call.

19
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.240
The app runs the tool and adds the result.

20
00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.030
Then the model answers, or

21
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:53.970
calls another tool.

22
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.830
Here’s a real call from Darsi Tiny,

23
00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.030
a small model on your phone.

24
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.170
I asked about the weather

25
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.510
in Kafi tomorrow.

26
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.120
It wrote the tool’s name, weather,

27
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.720
then the details in curly brackets:

28
00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:10.400
the place and the day.

29
00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.300
The model can’t run the tool:

30
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.940
it only writes text.

31
00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.750
The app runs it and adds the

32
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.330
result: sunny, thirty-five degrees,

33
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.490
a ten percent chance of rain.

34
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.640
Then the model answers from that result.

35
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:27.630
Quick check!

36
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.180
Who writes the tool call, and

37
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.120
who actually runs the tool?

38
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.620
The model writes the call,

39
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:37.980
and the app runs it.

40
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.690
So the app sees every call

41
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.450
before anything happens.

42
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.200
That’s where checks can go, and

43
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.460
you’ll write some in this course.

44
00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.890
Some jobs need two tools.

45
00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.270
Ask about a trip from Kafi

46
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.310
to Dala tomorrow.

47
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.000
Darsi Tiny calls the route tool

48
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.860
first: seventy kilometres.

49
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.070
Then it calls the weather tool for Dala,

50
00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.310
and gives one answer with both.

51
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.100
In Python, an app’s loop can be short.

52
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.850
Here a list stands in for the

53
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.910
model’s replies, because there’s

54
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.390
no model inside our code.

55
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.980
The loop runs a tool for each

56
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.060
call, and stops at the answer.

57
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.940
In the app, choose a request and

58
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.360
press One step until it answers.

59
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.700
Try the trip to Dala and count the tools.

60
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.320
Then open What Darsi Tiny sees: the

61
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.640
whole conversation is one text.

62
00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:35.550
Your turn.

63
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.880
On the trip to Dala, how many tools does

64
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.980
Darsi Tiny call before it answers?

65
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.740
Two: route, then weather.

66
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.840
Remember: an agent is a model

67
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.660
in a loop with tools.

68
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.520
A call is a tool’s name and its details.

69
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.900
The model writes, and the

70
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.260
app runs the tools.

71
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.260
Try it yourself: list three things you

72
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.660
asked a phone or an app to do this week.

73
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.410
For each one, was it only

74
00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.750
talk, or did it use a tool?

75
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.060
Then write the call it might have

76
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.200
made, with a name and details.
